submcu: collapse the sub-MCU timer loop

SM_UpdateTimer() runs exactly three iterations per call, always -- measured,
51,281,745 iterations over 17,093,915 calls with a single spike at 3 in the
histogram, and provable from the fact that sm.cycles only ever advances by 48
and sm.timer_cycles by 16. Most of those iterations only decrement a
prescaler. The loop is 44.3% of SM_Update, itself 11.5% of the program.

This computes the tick count and short-circuits the prescaler-only case rather
than walking it. GCC already hoists the enable and sleep loads and keeps
timer_cycles in a register, so what is left to remove is the prescaler and
counter memory round-trip.

Hand-counted Cortex-A53 instructions per call: 29 -> 18 on the fast exit a
real ROM takes, 56 -> 50 when all three ticks fire, and 29 -> 38 for three
prescaler-only ticks with no shortcut. Break-even is a fast-path hit rate
around 45%, which any prescaler reload of 3 or more clears.

Shipped after the gate was measured on real firmware. The effect depends on
the prescaler reload the firmware programs, which zero-filled ROMs drove to an
unrealistic 0-1; the real mk2 firmware programs 124 for 99.5% of a run, and
the fast path is eligible 96.3% of the time against a break-even of about 45%.
Measured on a real SC-55mk2 set: -2.70% of retired program instructions, and
bit-identical audio (digest b1e9a497433a81fa with and without).

Idempotency of the interrupt raise was verified rather than assumed:
INT_REQUEST is device_mode[0x1c], the three registers the loop reads are 0x1d,
0x1e and 0x1f, the loop body makes no calls and never reads INT_REQUEST back,
and both things that clear it run outside the loop.

tests/sm_timer_equivalence.cpp checks 6,374,220 cases with no ROMs --
exhaustive over small prescaler/counter/reload/tick combinations including
unaligned starts, exhaustive over enable x sleep x pre-set-INT_REQUEST, 3M
random cases over the full 8-bit ranges, and catch-ups of 65536 and 1000003
ticks. It compares final state, the exit point, and the index of the first
firing tick, so a collapsed interrupt raise would still be caught. Seven
deliberate bugs are all rejected.

Found by a subagent; patch and test verified here, recommendation followed.

+++ b/src/backend/submcu.cpp
@@ -1390,29 +1390,88 @@
     }
 }
 
+// The timer ticks once per 16 sub-MCU cycles.  SM_Update calls this after
+// every sub-MCU instruction, having just advanced sm.cycles by 48, and
+// sm.cycles is written nowhere else except SM_Reset (which zeroes it at
+// power-on, when timer_cycles is zero too).  The loop below therefore runs
+// exactly three times per call -- measured: 51,281,745 iterations over
+// 17,093,915 calls, and never any other count.
+//
+// Nothing the body touches can be observed between those iterations.  It makes
+// no calls; it never reads back sm.timer_cycles, and the only register it
+// writes, INT_REQUEST (0x1c), is distinct from the three it reads --
+// PRESCALER (0x1d), TIMER (0x1e) and TIMER_CTRL (0x1f) -- and is never read
+// inside the loop.  sm.sleep is not touched either.  So the whole tick count
+// can be worked out up front and the invariants read once:
+//
+//   * timer off (TIMER_CTRL bit 5) or asleep: the body is a no-op, only
+//     timer_cycles moves;
+//   * fewer ticks than the prescaler has left: the prescaler never reaches
+//     zero, so the counter cannot move and no interrupt can be raised.  This
+//     is the case a real ROM spends nearly all its time in.
+//
+// Otherwise the countdown still runs a tick at a time, but entirely in
+// registers, with the two reload registers hoisted and the interrupt raised
+// once at the end.  `|= 0x8` is idempotent and no iteration reads or clears
+// it, so raising it once is indistinguishable from raising it on every firing
+// tick -- proved in patches/tests/sm_timer_equivalence.cpp, which also checks
+// the index of the first firing tick, not merely the final flag.
+//
+// timer_cycles must land exactly where the original loop left it and not one
+// tick further: it persists across calls, so overshooting would silently
+// consume ticks that a later, finer prescaler setting would have made live.
+// That is the mistake patches/0001 made in draft and the test rejects it.
 void SM_UpdateTimer(submcu_t& sm)
 {
-    while (sm.timer_cycles < sm.cycles)
+    const uint64_t target = sm.cycles;
+    const uint64_t start = sm.timer_cycles;
+
+    if (start >= target)
+        return;
+
+    // The original adds 16 until timer_cycles >= target, so it runs
+    // ceil((target - start) / 16) times and stops exactly there.
+    const uint64_t ticks = (target - start + 15) >> 4;
+    sm.timer_cycles = start + (ticks << 4);
+
+    if ((sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_TIMER_CTRL] & 0x20) != 0 || sm.sleep)
+        return;
+
+    uint32_t prescaler = sm.timer_prescaler;
+    if (ticks <= prescaler)
+    {
+        sm.timer_prescaler = (uint8_t)(prescaler - (uint32_t)ticks);
+        return;
+    }
+
+    const uint32_t prescaler_reload = sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_PRESCALER];
+    const uint32_t counter_reload = sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_TIMER];
+    uint32_t counter = sm.timer_counter;
+    uint32_t fired = 0;
+    uint64_t remaining = ticks;
+
+    do
     {
-        if ((sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_TIMER_CTRL] & 0x20) == 0 && !sm.sleep)
+        if (prescaler == 0)
         {
-            if (sm.timer_prescaler == 0)
-            {
-                sm.timer_prescaler = sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_PRESCALER];
+            prescaler = prescaler_reload;
 
-                if (sm.timer_counter == 0)
-                {
-                    sm.timer_counter = sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_TIMER];
-                    sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_INT_REQUEST] |= 0x8;
-                }
-                else
-                    sm.timer_counter--;
+            if (counter == 0)
+            {
+                counter = counter_reload;
+                fired = 1;
             }
             else
-                sm.timer_prescaler--;
+                counter--;
         }
-        sm.timer_cycles += 16;
-    }
+        else
+            prescaler--;
+    } while (--remaining);
+
+    sm.timer_prescaler = (uint8_t)prescaler;
+    sm.timer_counter = (uint8_t)counter;
+    if (fired)
+        sm.device_mode[SM_DEV_INT_REQUEST] |= 0x8;
 }
 
